.TH "bathemit" 1 "@BATH_DATE@" "BATH @BATH_VERSION@" "BATH Manual"

.SH NAME
bathemit \- sample sequences from a profile HMM


.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bathemit
[\fIoptions\fR]
.I hmmfile


.SH DESCRIPTION

.PP
The
.B bathemit
program samples (emits) sequences from the profile HMM(s) in
.IR hmmfile ,
and writes them to output. Sampling sequences is useful for generating
synthetic test data and for inspecting what a built model actually
represents; it is not part of the search pipeline.

.PP
The default is to sample one unaligned sequence from the core
probability model, which means each sequence consists of one
full-length domain with no flanking sequence. Alternatively, with the
.B \-c
option, you can emit a simple majority-rule consensus sequence; with
.BR \-C ,
a consensus sequence annotated for per-position confidence; or with
.BR \-a ,
an alignment of several sampled sequences (in which case you probably
also want to set
.B \-N
to something other than its default of 1).

.PP
With the
.B \-p
option, you can instead sample from a fully configured search profile:
this includes nonhomologous flanking sequence, local/glocal alignment
mode, and the target length model, depending on the options chosen for
the profile.

.PP
With
.BR \-\-dna ,
the emitted amino acid sequence(s) are back-translated to DNA, one
randomly chosen synonymous codon per residue, using the genetic code
selected by
.BR \-\-ct .
This requires
.I hmmfile
to contain a protein profile. With
.BR \-\-fs ,
each codon is additionally, independently subject to a simulated
frameshift: instead of the usual 3 nucleotides, it may be rendered as
1, 2, 4, or 5, at the rate set by
.BR \-\-fsprob ,
using the same indel patterns scored by
.BR bathsearch 's
frameshift-aware algorithm. This is intended to generate realistic
test data for that algorithm.

.PP
.I hmmfile
may contain a library of profiles, in which case each one is used in
turn.

.PP
.I hmmfile
may be '\-' (dash), which means reading this input from stdin rather
than a file.


.SH OPTIONS

.TP
.B \-h
Help; print a brief reminder of command line usage and all available
options.

.TP
.BI \-o " <f>"
Direct the output sequences to file
.IR <f> ,
rather than to stdout.

.TP
.BI \-N " <n>"
Sample
.I <n>
sequences per model, rather than just one. Not compatible with
.B \-c
or
.BR \-C ,
which each always produce a single consensus sequence.

.TP
.BI \-\-seed " <n>"
Seed the random number generator with
.IR <n> ,
an integer >= 0. If
.I <n>
is nonzero, output is reproducible; the same command gives the same
result every time. If
.I <n>
is 0, the generator is seeded arbitrarily, and output varies from run
to run. The default is 0.


.SH OPTIONS CONTROLLING WHAT TO EMIT

The default is to sample
.I N
sequences from the core model. Alternatively, choose one (and only
one) of the following.

.TP
.B \-a
Sample
.I N
sequences from the core model, as in the default mode, and assemble
them into a multiple alignment rather than writing them out
individually. Because each sequence's alignment to the model is known
at the moment it is generated (that is how it was generated), no
realignment step is needed. Amino acid or DNA output (see
.BR \-\-dna ),
including with
.BR \-\-fs .

.TP
.B \-c
Emit a single, deterministic majority-rule consensus sequence, formed
by selecting the maximum-probability residue at each match state,
instead of sampling from the profile's probability distribution.

.TP
.B \-C
Emit a fancier consensus sequence than
.BR \-c ,
annotated for confidence. If the maximum-probability residue at a
position has probability p <
.BR \-\-minl ,
show it as an unspecified residue
.RI ( X
for amino acid,
.I N
for nucleic acid); if
.B \-\-minl
<= p <
.BR \-\-minu ,
show it in lower case; if p >=
.BR \-\-minu ,
show it in upper case. The default settings of
.B \-\-minl
and
.B \-\-minu
are both 0.0, which makes
.B \-C
give the same output as
.B \-c
unless you also set them.

.TP
.B \-p
Sample unaligned sequence(s) from the implicit search profile rather
than the core model. The core model consists only of the homologous
states (between the begin and end states). The profile adds the
nonhomologous N, C, and J states, local/glocal and uni/multihit
algorithm configuration, and the target length model, so sequences
sampled this way may include nonhomologous flanking sequence and more
than one homologous segment. By default the profile is configured in
multihit local mode with a target length of 400; see the options
below.


.SH OPTIONS CONTROLLING EMISSION FROM PROFILES WITH \-p

These options require
.BR \-p .

.TP
.BI \-L " <n>"
Configure the profile's target length model for a mean length of
approximately
.I <n>
rather than the default of 400.

.TP
.B \-\-local
Configure the profile for multihit local alignment. This is the
default.

.TP
.B \-\-unilocal
Configure the profile for unihit local alignment.

.TP
.B \-\-glocal
Configure the profile for multihit glocal alignment.

.TP
.B \-\-uniglocal
Configure the profile for unihit glocal alignment.


.SH OPTIONS CONTROLLING FANCY CONSENSUS EMISSION WITH \-C

These options require
.BR \-C .

.TP
.BI \-\-minl " <x>"
Set the
.I minl
threshold below which a position is shown as an unspecified residue.
.RI ( 0 " <= " x " <= " 1 ).
Default 0.0.

.TP
.BI \-\-minu " <x>"
Set the
.I minu
threshold at or above which a position is shown in upper case.
.RI ( 0 " <= " x " <= " 1 ).
Default 0.0.


.SH OPTIONS CONTROLLING DNA OUTPUT

.TP
.B \-\-dna
Back-translate the emitted amino acid sequence(s) to DNA, choosing one
synonymous codon uniformly at random per residue, using the genetic
code selected by
.BR \-\-ct .
Requires
.I hmmfile
to contain a protein profile.

.TP
.BI \-\-ct " <n>"
With
.BR \-\-dna ,
use the alternate genetic code specified by NCBI translation table
number
.IR <n> ,
instead of the default of 1 (the standard genetic code).

.TP
.B \-\-fs
With
.BR \-\-dna ,
independently subject each codon to a simulated frameshift, at the
rate set by
.BR \-\-fsprob .
A frameshifted codon is rendered as 1, 2, 4, or 5 nucleotides instead
of the usual 3. Not compatible with
.B \-c
or
.BR \-C ,
which each always produce a single deterministic sequence.

.TP
.BI \-\-fsprob " <x>"
With
.BR \-\-fs ,
set the per-codon frameshift rate to
.IR <x> .
The default matches the fixed rate used internally by BATH's own
frameshift-aware scoring model.
.RI ( 0.001 " <= " x " <= " 0.1 ).


.SH SEE ALSO

.BR bathsearch (1),
.BR bathbuild (1),
.BR bathfetch (1),
.BR bathconvert (1),
.BR bathstat (1),
.BR bathalign (1)

.PP
For complete documentation, see the BATH repository:
https://github.com/TravisWheelerLab/BATH


